class.description:
[XMLElement] is a lightweight, lazily created view of one element in the tree of an [XML] resource. It stores no copy: reads show the tree and writes (name, text, attributes, children) change the tree of the [XML] resource. A change drops the text kept by [method TextDataFormat.parse], so comments and formatting are lost on the next save.
Get the root element with [method XML.get_root] or [method XML.create_root]. Create a new element with [code]XMLElement.new()[/code] and attach it with [method add_child].
Paths of [method find_first] and [method find_all] are element names separated by [code]/[/code], relative to this element ([code]"items/item"[/code]).
[codeblocks]
[gdscript]
var xml = XML.new()
xml.parse("<shop><item id=\"1\">Sword</item></shop>")
var item = XMLElement.new()
item.name = "item"
item.set_attribute("id", "2")
item.text = "Shield"
xml.get_root().add_child(item)
print(xml.find_all("item").size()) # Prints 2
[/gdscript]
[csharp]
var xml = new Xml();
xml.Parse("<shop><item id=\"1\">Sword</item></shop>");
var item = new Godot.XmlElement();
item.Name = "item";
item.SetAttribute("id", "2");
item.Text = "Shield";
xml.GetRoot().AddChild(item);
GD.Print(xml.FindAll("item").Count); // Prints 2
[/csharp]
[/codeblocks]
[b]Note:[/b] In C# the class is [code]XmlElement[/code], which clashes with [code]System.Xml.XmlElement[/code] when both namespaces are imported. Use the qualified name [code]Godot.XmlElement[/code].
[b]Note:[/b] The whole tree is kept in memory. For huge files use [XMLParser] and [XMLWriter].
